        "abstract": "In envisioned non-terrestrial networks (NTNs), using optical links for the satellite's backhaul connectivity is a widespread solution to provide high data rates while using spectrum efficiently and keeping interference at a minimum. For the satellite platform, the provision of accurate attitude knowledge and control is mandatory, as optical antennas usually have to be steered precisely toward their communication partner. Newly developed or produced satellites should undergo intense on-ground verification to increase reliability. We present a distributed hardware-in-the-loop (HiL) simulation environment based on highly accurate turn tables (TTs). We present a performance evaluation based on a real mission scenario, the QUBE 3U-CubeSat mission. We can hold the introduced attitude deviation of the testbed relative to the simulated dynamic below <tex xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">$0.01 3^{} (1\\sigma)$</tex> with a rotation rate deviation under <tex xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\">$0.03 0/\\mathrm{s}(1\\sigma^{})$</tex>. We address challenges with distributed testbeds, like time synchronization and data exchange between the testbed components and the device-under-test (DUT).",
